WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.838
Open a map on your phone, and in seconds it knows where you are, almost to the metre.

2
00:00:06.158 --> 00:00:07.485
How does it do that?

3
00:00:08.035 --> 00:00:13.779
About thirty GPS satellites orbit the Earth, around twenty thousand kilometres above us.

4
00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:17.848
Each one carries an extremely precise atomic clock.

5
00:00:18.398 --> 00:00:26.191
Every satellite constantly broadcasts a signal saying which satellite it is, and exactly what time the signal was sent.

6
00:00:26.741 --> 00:00:31.172
Your phone receives that signal a tiny fraction of a second later.

7
00:00:31.492 --> 00:00:37.791
Since the signal travels at the speed of light, the delay tells your phone how far away the satellite is.

8
00:00:38.341 --> 00:00:44.985
One distance isn't enough, but with three satellites there's only one point where all the distances match.

9
00:00:45.305 --> 00:00:50.659
A fourth satellite corrects the error of your phone's clock, which is far less accurate.

10
00:00:51.209 --> 00:00:54.158
The timing has to be incredibly precise.

11
00:00:54.478 --> 00:01:00.303
An error of just one millionth of a second means a mistake of about three hundred metres.

12
00:01:00.853 --> 00:01:09.262
There's even a twist from Einstein: the clocks on the satellites run faster than ours, by about thirty-eight microseconds a day.

13
00:01:09.582 --> 00:01:17.930
Engineers adjust them. Without that correction, GPS positions would drift by around ten kilometres every day.

14
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:24.554
So every time you follow a map, you're using atomic clocks, space, and Einstein's relativity.
